The loss census of section 1
==============================================================================
  Section 1 attaches 'thermalisation about 30% of incident power' and
  'sub-bandgap transmission about 20%' to the SQ optimum E_g = 1.34 eV.

  E_g (eV)      sub-bandgap   thermalisation    left over
  1.34                29.9%            23.2%        46.9%
  1.12                19.2%            31.7%        49.1%
  2.25                73.6%             4.4%        22.0%

  The quoted pair, 30% thermalisation and 20% sub-bandgap, is the pair
  for E_g = 1.12 eV (31.7% and 19.2%), that is, for silicon.  At the
  1.34 eV the sentence names, the two are close to the other way
  round: 23.2% thermalisation against 29.9% sub-bandgap.  The
  conclusion the paragraph draws - that both losses are large and that
  a single gap cannot avoid either - is unaffected.

### section 6.2: the photocurrent integral
==============================================================================
The multiplication factor of the high-energy channel
==============================================================================
  eta_SF x eta_DET x EQE_BHJ = 1.85 x 0.92 x 0.90 = 1.5318
  section 6.2 calls this EQE_high = 153% in the blue-green: correct

==============================================================================
The two channels of section 6.2
==============================================================================
                                                  measured   claimed
  IR channel, 1.12-2.25 eV, at EQE = 0.85           28.89     28.50
  SF channel, above 2.25 eV, at EQE = 1.53          14.95     23.80
  total J_sc, mA/cm^2                               43.83     52.30

  The infrared channel is right: 28.5 claimed against 28.89 measured,
  an error of 1.3%, which is within the accuracy of reading a
  spectrum off a chart.

  The high-energy channel is not.  Photons above 2.25 eV carry
  9.76 mA/cm^2 of charge if every one of them makes exactly one
  electron.  The claimed 23.8 mA/cm^2 therefore needs
    EQE_high = 23.8 / 9.76 = 244%
  that is, 2.44 electrons per absorbed photon.
  Singlet fission gives at most two.  The claim exceeds the ceiling of
  its own mechanism by 22%, and exceeds the 153% it states by 59%.

  Where the number could come from: 23.8 mA/cm^2 at EQE_high = 1.53
  is the current of all photons above 1.96 eV, that is above 634 nm,
  rather than above the 2.25 eV / 551 nm the same sentence
  specifies - a shift of 294 meV.

==============================================================================
What the architecture's current can be
==============================================================================
  the preprint's own efficiencies                   43.83 mA/cm^2
  perfect transfer and collection, eta_SF = 1.85    52.04 mA/cm^2
  perfect everything, two triplets per singlet      53.50 mA/cm^2

  For comparison, a plain 1.12 eV absorber with EQE = 1 and no fission
  at all collects 43.81 mA/cm^2.
  With the preprint's own quantum efficiencies the whole
  fission-plus-Dexter cascade returns 43.83 mA/cm^2, which is the same
  number to within 0.1%: the multiplication pays for the 0.85, 0.92
  and 0.90 and nothing more.  The gain over silicon in this
  architecture is not in the current; it is in collecting that current
  at a voltage set by the 1.12 eV gap while the 0.85 and 0.90 losses
  are absorbed by the doubling.

==============================================================================
Section 6.2's efficiency, recomputed
==============================================================================
                                                         J_sc      PCE
  as published                                          52.30   35.58%
  with J_sc from the preprint's own efficiencies        43.83   29.82%
  with perfect fission, transfer and collection         53.50   36.40%

  The published arithmetic is internally consistent:
    52.3 x 0.81 x 0.840 / 100 = 0.3558, printed as 35.58%
  It is the current entering it that the spectrum does not support.

==============================================================================
Can 220 nm of active layer absorb both bands?
==============================================================================
  Section 1 gives alpha ~ 1e5 /cm for organic absorbers; table 1 gives
  a single 220 nm active layer holding both the fission donor and the
  infrared acceptor, with a back reflector behind it.  Each absorber
  therefore sees only its own share of the thickness.

  acceptor       its path    single pass   with reflector
  30        %         66 nm          48.3%            73.3%
  40        %         88 nm          58.5%            82.8%
  50        %        110 nm          66.7%            88.9%
  60        %        132 nm          73.3%            92.9%
  70        %        154 nm          78.6%            95.4%

  To reach the 0.85 the infrared channel assumes at an even 50:50 split,
  double pass, needs alpha >= 8.62e+04 /cm over the whole band.
  To reach the 0.90 the fission channel assumes at an even 50:50 split,
  double pass, needs alpha >= 1.05e+05 /cm over the whole band.

  At the peak of an organic absorption band 1e5 /cm is ordinary, so
  both numbers are reachable there.  The requirement is that alpha
  stay near its peak across 1.12-2.25 eV for the acceptor, a 556 nm
  wide window ending at its own absorption edge, where alpha of any
  real absorber falls away.  This is not quantified here; it is
  flagged as the assumption the 0.85 rests on.

### the 45.9% limit, against the model it is cited from
==============================================================================
Three self-checks before anything is concluded
==============================================================================
  Shockley-Queisser, one carrier per photon
    optimum at E_g = 1.335 eV, efficiency 33.77%
    published value: 33.7% at 1.34 eV
    at 1.34 eV exactly: 33.76%, J_sc 35.02 mA/cm^2, V_oc 1.084 V, FF 0.890

  Carrier multiplication, unlimited: QY = floor(E / E_g)
    optimum at E_g = 0.70 eV, efficiency 44.62%
    reference [5]: 44.4% at 0.70 eV

  Carrier multiplication capped at two per photon, which is what
  singlet fission does, with a free doubling threshold
    optimum at E_g = 0.94 eV, threshold 1.88 eV, efficiency 42.09%
    same group, NREL/CP-590-38992: 42.0% at 0.95 eV

  Three independent published values reproduced to better than
  0.3 percentage points.  The model below is the same code.

==============================================================================
The limit of the HOS-PV 2.0 architecture
==============================================================================
  A single junction extracting at E_g(IR) = 1.12 eV, one electron per
  photon between 1.12 and 2.25 eV, two above 2.25 eV.

                                                             limit
  plain single gap at 1.12 eV                               33.48%
  Shockley-Queisser optimum, 1.34 eV                        33.76%
  this architecture, at its own two energies                41.24%
  best possible with a cap of two per photon                42.09%
  published claim                                           45.90%

  So the central idea works.  A single junction at 1.12 eV that
  doubles above 2.25 eV does beat Shockley-Queisser, by 7.5 points,
  and its J_sc of 53.6 mA/cm^2 and V_oc of 0.883 V are where the gain
  comes from.  Exceeding the limit is not the problem.

  The claimed figure is the problem.  45.9% is above the best that
  doubling can do at any pair of energies whatsoever (42.09%), so no
  choice of donor or acceptor reaches it, and it is 4.7 points above
  the limit at the energies the preprint actually specifies.

  Where 45.9% comes from is visible in the source it cites.
  Reference [5] reports, in its abstract:
    single gap, no multiplication                  33.7%
    single gap, with multiplication                44.4%
    ideal TWO-gap tandem, without and with CM      45.7% to 47.7%
  45.9% is not the single-junction row.  It falls inside the two-gap
  tandem range, in a paper whose single-junction answer is 44.4% for
  unlimited multiplication and 42.0% for the doubling that singlet
  fission actually performs.  The architecture's one selling point is
  that it is a single junction.

==============================================================================
V_oc, FF and the non-radiative loss
==============================================================================
  radiative-limit V_oc at E_g = 1.12 eV
    plain single gap                     0.8782 V
    with the doubling above 2.25 eV      0.8834 V
    section 6.2 states                   0.9000 V
    difference                           17 mV, in the claim's favour

  V_oc = V_oc,rad - dV_nr, with the preprint's dV_nr = 0.09 V:
    from its own 0.90 V      0.90 - 0.09 = 0.810 V, printed as 0.81
    from the measured limit  0.8834 - 0.09 = 0.793 V

  What dV_nr = 0.09 V requires.  By the reciprocity relation
  dV_nr = -(kT/q) ln EQE_EL:
    dV_nr = 0.22 V  ->  EQE_EL = 1.91e-04   (achiral NFA blends, per section 6.2)
    dV_nr = 0.09 V  ->  EQE_EL = 3.01e-02   (claimed for HOS-PV 2.0)
  The step from 0.22 V to 0.09 V is a factor 158 in
  electroluminescence quantum efficiency, from 0.02% to 3%.

  Fill factor: the ideal-diode ceiling at V_oc = 0.81 V and n = 1 is
  86.2% (Green's expression).  The claimed 84.0% is below it, so the
  fill factor is the one headline number in section 6.2 that is
  reachable as stated, though no organic cell has reached it.

  Finally, how hard the claimed operating point is relative to its
  own limit:
    HOS-PV 2.0 against the measured limit         86.3% of limit
    HOS-PV 2.0 against its claimed limit          77.5% of limit
    c-Si record against Auger limit               92.9% of limit
    best OPV today against SQ                     59.3% of limit
  The projection asks an organic blend to reach a larger fraction of
  its radiative limit than silicon has managed after fifty years.

### sections 3 and 4: CISS and the Pauli spin blockade
==============================================================================
The published arithmetic of section 4.1
==============================================================================
  1 - eta_PB P^2 = 1 - 0.95 x 0.85^2 = 0.3136
  section 4.1 states beta_eff = 0.314 beta_L and 68.6% suppression
  measured: beta_eff = 0.3136 beta_L, suppression 68.64%
  correct as written.

==============================================================================
eta_PB against the two times the same section gives for it
==============================================================================
  eta_PB = exp(-tau_ext / tau_s), with tau_s = 5 us and
  tau_ext = 20 to 80 ns, both from section 4.1 itself.

  tau_ext          eta_PB  beta_eff/beta_L    suppression
       20 ns      0.9960           0.2804          72.0%
       80 ns      0.9841           0.2890          71.1%

  The stated eta_PB = 0.95 corresponds to tau_ext = 256 ns, not to
  the 20-80 ns the same sentence gives.  The error is in the
  preprint's own disfavour: its own times give eta_PB = 0.984 to
  0.996 and a suppression of 72.0% rather than 68.6%, so the
  conclusion of section 4.1 survives this correction and improves.

==============================================================================
But tau_ext follows from the mobility, which is the weak point
==============================================================================
  Section 4.1 defines tau_ext = d^2 / (2 mu V_int).  With the 220 nm
  active layer of table 1 and V_int = 0.8 V, the transit time is set
  by the mobility - and section 1 introduces organic blends as having
  'low carrier mobilities' without giving a value.

  mu (cm^2/Vs)       tau_ext     eta_PB  beta_eff/beta_L    suppression
  1.0e-04            3025 ns     0.5461           0.6055          39.5%
  3.2e-04             957 ns     0.8259           0.4033          59.7%
  1.0e-03             302 ns     0.9413           0.3199          68.0%
  3.2e-03              96 ns     0.9811           0.2912          70.9%
  1.0e-02              30 ns     0.9940           0.2819          71.8%

  At the bottom of the range, where a disordered blend actually sits,
  the transit time is 3.0 us, comparable with the 5 us spin
  lifetime rather than negligible against it, and the suppression
  falls to 39.5%.  The 20-80 ns quoted in section 4.1 needs
    tau_ext =  20 ns  ->  mu = 1.5e-02 cm^2/Vs
    tau_ext =  80 ns  ->  mu = 3.8e-03 cm^2/Vs
  which is one to two orders above a disordered bulk heterojunction
  and at or beyond the best ordered organic films.  So the
  mechanism's margin, tau_ext << tau_s, is real at high mobility and
  gone at low mobility, and the preprint asserts the high end while
  its introduction argues the low end.

==============================================================================
The spin statistics the suppression is measured against
==============================================================================
  Section 1 states that uncorrelated encounters form 25% singlet and
  75% triplet, and section 4.1 blocks the triplet channel.  If the
  triplet channel is blocked, what is left is the singlet fraction:
    achiral device, spin statistics alone      beta_eff = 0.250 beta_L
    section 4.1, chiral, P = 0.85              beta_eff = 0.314 beta_L
  The chiral result is the larger of the two.  Unless beta_L is read
  as the spin-blind Langevin coefficient - in which case an ordinary
  achiral blend should already show 0.250 - the expression as written
  does not improve on the statistics it replaces.  Reference [3],
  Rao et al. 2013, which the preprint cites for the triplet drain, is
  precisely the measurement of this spin control in achiral blends.

==============================================================================
What P_CISS = tanh(dE / 2kT) commits the design to
==============================================================================
  Read as written, the polarisation is a thermal population ratio, so
  a polarisation measured at 300 K fixes the splitting:

  P_CISS          required dE (meV)
  0.75                      50.0
  0.85                      64.5
  0.90                      75.7

  Holding that splitting fixed at 64.5 meV, the same formula then
  predicts how the whole mechanism behaves with temperature - and a
  solar cell does not run at 25 C:

  T (degC)         P_CISS  beta_eff/beta_L    suppression
  0                 0.879           0.2728          72.7%
  25                0.850           0.3199          68.0%
  45                0.827           0.3569          64.3%
  59                0.810           0.3821          61.8%
  85                0.780           0.4271          57.3%

  This is a prediction, and a testable one: the suppression weakens
  as the module heats up and strengthens in the cold.  Section 4.2
  claims the opposite advantage, better low-irradiance winter
  behaviour, for unrelated reasons; this is the cleaner signature.

==============================================================================
xi_SOC proportional to Z^4
==============================================================================
  Section 1 justifies the weak spin-orbit coupling of carbon with
  'xi_SOC proportional to Z^4'.  Z^4 is the hydrogenic fine-structure
  scaling, for an electron in a bare nuclear potential.  The
  electrons that carry spin in a conjugated polymer are valence p
  electrons, screened, and the exponent can be measured: the ground
  term splitting of the halogens is exactly one valence np shell
  stepped down one column of the periodic table.

  element  Z      2P(3/2)-2P(1/2) (1/cm)
  F        9                 404.141
  Cl       17                882.351
  Br       35               3685.240
  I        53               7602.976

  least-squares fit of ln(splitting) against ln(Z):
    measured exponent          1.69
    claimed in section 1       4.0
    valence scaling in the review literature  2.0
    worst fit residual         20%

  So the exponent is 1.69, below even the Z^2 the review literature
  gives for outermost electrons, and far from 4.  It does not change
  the conclusion: from carbon to lead the measured trend still gives
    measured         factor        83 between Z = 6 and Z = 82
    Z^2              factor       187 between Z = 6 and Z = 82
    Z^4 as claimed   factor     34886 between Z = 6 and Z = 82
  Carbon's advantage is real whichever exponent is used.  Only the
  size of it, and the law quoted for it, are wrong.

### sections 2.2 and 5, and table 1: the materials
==============================================================================
The energy ordering of section 2.2, with the preprint's own numbers
==============================================================================
  Section 2.1 requires   E(S1) >= 2 E(T1)
  Section 2.2 requires   E(S1) >= 2 E(T1) >= 2 E_CT >= 2 E_g(IR)
  Section 2.2 specifies  E(S1) = 2.25 eV, E(T1) = 1.15 eV,
                         E_g(IR) = 1.12 eV

  2 E(T1) = 2.30 eV against E(S1) = 2.25 eV:
    the first link fails by 50 meV.  The fission the architecture
    rests on is endothermic by 50 meV, which is 1.9 kT at 25 C,
    not exothermic as section 2.1 requires.
  2 E(T1) = 2.30 eV against 2 E_g(IR) = 2.24 eV:
    this link holds, with 60 meV to spare.

  Endothermic fission is not fatal - tetracene is endothermic and
  still efficient - but section 2.1 also demands 1/k_SF below 100 fs
  to outrun singlet decay, and sub-100 fs fission is what exothermic
  chromophores do.  The two requirements pull opposite ways.

  What the ordering demands of a real chromophore:
    E(T1) >= E_g(IR) = 1.12 eV   and   E(S1) >= 2 E(T1) >= 2.24 eV

  chromophore     E(S1)    E(T1)     2T1-S1  T1 >= 1.12?  S1 >= 2 T1?
  pentacene        2.20     0.86      -0.48           no          yes
  tetracene        2.32     1.25       0.18          yes           no
  rubrene          2.21     1.14       0.07          yes           no
  the preprint     2.25     1.15       0.05          yes           no

  Each of the three measured chromophores fails one of the two
  conditions, and so do the preprint's own numbers.  Pentacene, the
  one that fissions in 80 fs, has E(T1) = 0.86 eV: it cannot feed a
  1.12 eV acceptor at all, because its triplets lie below the gap.
  Rubrene comes closest and misses by 70 meV.  Table 1's named
  donor, TIPS-tetracene, is a tetracene: endothermic, picoseconds.
  The window is narrow rather than empty, and nothing in the
  preprint is measured inside it.

==============================================================================
Layer 6: the altermagnetic collector
==============================================================================
  Table 1 specifies oriented RuO2(101) or MnTe, 40 nm, with a spin
  splitting of ~0.8 eV; section 5.1 says 0.5-1.0 eV.

  The splitting is right.  Angle-resolved photoemission on MnTe
  measures 0.37 eV and 0.80 eV in two independent studies, so the
  0.8 eV in table 1 is the published number for the material named.
  Reference [7], Smejkal, Sinova and Jungwirth, Phys. Rev. X 12,
  040501 (2022), is the right citation for altermagnetism, correctly
  attached, and the symmetry argument in section 5.1 - rotation
  rather than translation or inversion, hence spin-split bands where
  a collinear antiferromagnet has none - is stated correctly.

  What is not stated is the temperature at which it stops.

                                        T (K)
  MnTe Neel temperature, measured       267 - 307
  standard test conditions             298.15
  typical module NOCT                  318.15
  measured NOCT, thin film             332.15
  IEC 61215 upper qualification        358.15

  A module in the field runs above MnTe's ordering temperature on
  both published values.  At the 332 K measured for a thin-film
  module under the IEC 61215 method, the collector is 25 to 65 K
  into its paramagnetic phase, where the spin splitting that layer 6
  exists for is zero.  Even standard test conditions, 298.15 K, sit
  at or above the lower published value.

  The alternative named first, RuO2, has the opposite problem: its
  ordering temperature is well above room temperature, but whether
  it is magnetically ordered at all has been contested since 2024.
  Muon spin rotation and neutron diffraction put the bulk moment
  below 1.4e-4 muB/Ru, with the dissent coming from thin films and
  surfaces - which is the form table 1 specifies, so the question is
  open rather than closed against it.  Either way, the two materials
  offered for layer 6 are the one whose magnetism is disputed and
  the one that orders below the cell's operating temperature.

==============================================================================
Table 2: specific power of at least 10,000 W/kg
==============================================================================
  Layer by layer, at mid-range for the three assumed values:

  layer                             g/m^2      share
  1 substrate, PEN                 68.000      56.3%
  2 ITO anode                       0.714       0.6%
  3 c-SAM hole layer                0.002       0.0%
  4 active layer                    0.264       0.2%
  5 MgO tunnel barrier              0.004       0.0%
  6 altermagnet, RuO2               0.279       0.2%
  6 back reflector, Ag              1.573       1.3%
  7 encapsulation                  50.000      41.4%
  total                           120.836

  The 50 um substrate and the 25 um encapsulation are 97.7% of the
  mass and do no physics.  Everything that does - the two absorbers,
  the electrodes, the barrier and the altermagnet - is 2.84 g/m^2.

  Specific power, across the full range of the three assumptions:

                                        g/m^2           W/kg
  lightest case, PCE as claimed         115.3           3087
  heaviest case, PCE as claimed         126.4           2815
  mid case, PCE as claimed              120.8           2944
  mid case, PCE recomputed              120.8           2468
  claimed in table 2                        -          10000

  The claim is short by a factor 3.4 at every corner of the sweep.
  To reach 10,000 W/kg at the claimed 35.6% the whole cell must
  weigh 35.6 g/m^2.  The functional stack already weighs 2.84 g/m^2,
  so the packaging budget is 32.7 g/m^2 against the 118.0 g/m^2 that
  table 1's 75 um of polymer actually weighs.
  At a mean packaging density of 1.68 g/cm^3 that is 19 um of
  polymer in total, against the 75 um of table 1.  The claim
  is reachable for a free-standing ultrathin cell, which is where
  the 5,000-10,000 W/kg figures in that column come from; it is not
  reachable for the stack in table 1.

==============================================================================
What 52.3 mA/cm^2 costs the rest of the stack
==============================================================================
  A higher current makes every series resistance matter more.  The
  fill-factor loss from series resistance is about
  dFF/FF = R_s J_sc / V_oc, so the budget for layers 2, 3 and 5
  together, to hold the claimed FF to within one point, is:

  J_sc (mA/cm^2)            R_s for 1 point   R_s for 5 points
  42.8                      0.189 ohm.cm^2    0.946 ohm.cm^2
  52.3                      0.155 ohm.cm^2    0.774 ohm.cm^2

  The 42.8 mA/cm^2 row is the silicon cell in table 2, for scale.
  The whole stack above the absorber - a 100 nm ITO film, a 1.5 nm
  chiral monolayer and a 1.2 nm MgO tunnel barrier in series - has
  to come in under 0.15 ohm.cm^2.  Nothing here says it cannot; the
  point is that the claimed current tightens that budget by a
  further 22% relative to a silicon cell, while adding two layers
  in series that a silicon cell does not have.

